editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7053:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/review-cold.md:15:The person who matters is Cael, but Lira is the heart of the first half, and the movement is honest enough to let her be: her grin at the closed seam, "Third one someday", the thumbnail marks in the chair wax. Brom is the surprise; he is given the best single piece of advice in the book ("Like somebody with something in his pocket") and the best refusal to perform (the hard chair, "That was horrible").
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7054:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/review-cold.md:86:Observation to keep: the sixth question, the brace, "Records of what? Ask it," and the chin dipping over a receipt. The prepared refusal is never spent and the page knows that is the point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7070:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/review-editorial.md:55:2. **Lira's hip ten days old.** ch51 l. 9 "Ten days. The oil on Fiske's boards had been ten days ago." Semifinal the ninth (M6 ruling) → the nineteenth: verified. "Nine days" of no sparring from the tenth (M6): verified. Four days off after the final (ch53 l. 11): verified against the packet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7102:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:235:The three exercises were inside, in the driest hand he owned, every word of them true and none of them complete, each with a tick beside its title and two figures in its margin and an initial on the tab, as the wing marked everything. Something he had never handed in lay against the back board. A slip of paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7111:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-56.md:87:"Nine days he's been on this hill, and not a soul has played to him. Withrow stood still on purpose. Bracken's file stood up without anybody holding it. Rooke signed his sheets and went back to his cohort. Yesterday Gault ran three hours of true instruments and wrote down a flat line and stood up for it. The whole house has spent nine days being exactly what it is, as hard as it can." He spread his hands on the table. "So picture me walking in there and playing to him. Then I'm the only performer on the whole bluff, and I've spoilt the single thing this house has managed to let him see."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7112:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-56.md:95:Karis took the grey notebook out of her satchel, opened it at a fresh page, and wrote, in her small upright hand, *Refusal in the open. Reason: good. Also convenient.* She turned it round so that he could read it, and he nodded, and she closed the book again with the air of somebody filing a document in a box that nobody would be allowed to open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7113:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-56.md:205:Four true answers lay to hand. Three would have opened every door he had kept bolted all his life. The fourth was complete and honest and opened none of them, and it was waiting for him, written two years ago inside a notebook's front board for some reason that had nothing to do with today.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7148:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:47:Karis had the marbled book open beside her bowl. When Lira sat down, Karis reached under her chair without a word and brought up the two practice batons, still tied together in the middle with Lira's own hip bandage, in the ugly knot Lira had pulled tight nine days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7149:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:57:"Nine days," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7150:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:59:"Nine days," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7151:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:63:For nine days they had not sparred, or talked about the bout, or watched each other cross a room. It had not been politeness. Without the silence, each of them would have spent nine days building a fight out of the other one's wound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7152:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:67:And he knew hers. If either of them had spent the last nine days thinking about the other's number, they would have walked out today to fight a ledger instead of a person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7153:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:143:For nine days he had kept the read at its thinnest layer, because the reach at depth and the hold were paid out of one purse, and the purse was shallow. This afternoon, though, no one on the bluff would spare him a look. Every eye in the yard, the Archmarshal's among them, would be on the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7154:editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-51.md:269:She knew his number, and he knew hers. And for nine days each of them had refused to build one single thought on the other's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7155:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:35:*Things that are going to happen whether I schedule them or not.* He had written that three nights ago, in Hesk's book, under the trial. He had thought then that he was being dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7156:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:45:Bracken brought the west tower's half of it to the long table at midday, with the pouch under his arm and the expression of a man who has been given a very good piece of gossip by a colleague and intends to hand it on with every courtesy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7157:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:95:Rooke was at the back of the crowd with his hands deep in his coat pockets, and said nothing. Seln had gone up three steps of the west tower's porch, out of the press, with the travel pouch held flat against his chest, and was reading the board from there with exactly the expression he used for a revised timetable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7158:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-48.md:113:Withrow sat at the head of it with a pot of tea at her elbow. Rooke had the chair at her right, Bracken the chair at her left with the pouch on his knees. Gault was at the far end peeling an apple with great attention. Ephram sat between Brom and Karis. Seln had the last chair, the one nearest the door, where he always sat when he sat with them at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7165:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:126:- Hesk's book: the ring entry (protected 27 plus one line); the minute; protected 28.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7166:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/AUTHOR-REPORT.md:127:- The old volume's last leaves: the steward; the squall line; the toast and its margin; the second slip's log.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7169:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:5:The house was quiet in the way a house is quiet when everybody in it has gone somewhere else at once. The squad was at the hired hall. Rooke was with them. Gault had gone to the healers' corridor at the Concourse to look at the station that would stand at the ring's north break. Bracken was at the west tower with the pouch. On the stair the keeper's cat lay in a square of sun, and down in the courtyard somebody's washing moved on a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7170:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:189:He wrote the minute into Hesk's book while they watched, under the ring, in his best hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7171:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:231:He wrote it in the old volume, in its very last leaves, in the smallest hand he owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7172:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-53.md:253:He opened Hesk's book under the lamp, past the ring and the minute, to the next clean leaf, and wrote slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7174:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/review-editorial.md:138:Six confirmed plus the anomaly ("The still place from session nine, untouched in its three words"). No new capability. **Reading the medium** is a retasking of the Iron-adjacent read and the page says so ("the read that had read weight all his life … He did not reach for her this time. He reached the other way"): twelve late in ch47; the squall's front early twice with the powder wharf's masts humming (ch50); ten in ten "a half-beat before it reached him, off the air, with her body put away" (ch52), timed on Hesk's clock in Karis's column and bounded by her in the right words ("At Iron. At Lira. … I don't know what it is at anything else, and nor do you."). The price appears only as narrowing, twice, with Karis's mark beside it "for things she meant to come back to". The Gold-scale price is not known (§3B item 9 ✓). **The bank** is named as the packet's B4-reconciliation requires: Wind past six "and the bill comes in the morning"; the push "from the Bronze at Ardenmere, at a fighting load, past the four blows a sitting" (Feryn, by the audio rule); "Reydan's give, on a scored floor in front of a crowd, which it hasn't been since the bout at Ardenmere, and that bout's the only paper it's on" (not "first time in its existence"); "Karis's spark past the two contacts we allow in public"; the read "as far down as it'll go". The minute is written before, in Hesk's book under the ring entry, and the B4 three-rule minute is restated in Karis's line ("Nothing made to happen. Only inside a fight somebody else begins. The cost on paper first."). **Shadow sealed**, priced aloud by Lira as presence-suppression and movement-masking without those words, and kept for the debt; "a year ago, near enough" (C9). §7.1's line is verbatim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7175:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/review-editorial.md:215:| ch48 s3 | "which was where she had been at the cheap slate in the circuit house at Ardenmere, and at every board since" · "Ephram had been standing exactly so at every board since the cold term, and had never said a word about it to anybody" · "Seln had gone up three steps of the west tower's porch, out of the press, with the travel pouch held flat against his chest, and was reading the board from there" · "She had looked at this board somewhere earlier, alone, and then decided whose it was. It was theirs. She had given it to them." · "the four bracket finals stood waiting, the titles the continent had fought over longest, set lower and printed smaller than a bout whose result would go into nobody's standings at all" | "Lira at his left shoulder where she had stood at every board since Ardenmere" · "Ephram at the outside … having appointed himself to that job at some point in the winter without mentioning it to anyone" · "Seln was at the colonnade's edge, forty feet off, holding the delegation pouch, reading the board with the rest of the city" · "Withrow was not there … the chancellor had decided this particular reading belonged to the five of them" · "The finals below it, the four bracket titles of the oldest competition on the continent, in smaller type than a bout worth zero points" (the whole scene runs the source's beat order: walk down by custom, the crowd's noise, the line, the formation, Seln, Rooke, Withrow, the bracketed word, the protected line) |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7180:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-47.md:135:He did not hurry, and he did not look up at the crowd, not once. He unlocked the case and opened the book and read the last entry and looked at the frame and checked one against the other, with the rain darkening the shoulders of his coat, and shut the book and locked the case and went and stood against the wall with his hands behind his back. When the hour was over he took his keys and left. Cael thought it was the best piece of ordinary work he had seen anybody do all sitting, and he wrote it down that night in the old volume's last leaves, in four words, because the old volume was running out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7182:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-52.md:49:Cael wrote the toast down within the hour, word for word, at the corner of the long table in the old volume's last leaves. Then he sat over it for a while and gave it a few lines of its own underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7183:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-52.md:269:He stayed. The light was nearly gone. He sat down on the edge of the oak round, with the four masts standing round him in the dusk, and took Hesk's book out of his coat and wrote in it on his knee, because this one would not keep till he got home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7193:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-50.md:39:Withrow sat on his left and Rooke on his right, with Bracken behind them against the wall, holding the pouch. Across the table Auremont's delegation head sat with his hands folded, a tall greying man, heavy in the shoulders, with the expression of somebody who has come to do a thing properly and does not expect it to work. There was an empty chair beside him. Then there was a gap the width of a chair, which nobody had arranged and everybody could see, and then Daeva.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/state/movement-003/recheck-r2.stdout.log:7194:editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/state/movement-008/pre-repair/chapter-50.md:216:He did not have an answer for that, so he did not give one. They went in along the wall together with the rain coming down properly now, and halfway back Lira started laughing at nothing, at the wet and the dark and the squall, and after a while he was laughing too. But he wrote it down that night in the old volume, under the four words about the steward, in the smallest hand he had.
