     1	# Chapter 50 — Within the Bell
     2	
     3	The rule was older than the wool exchange, and the house's counsel explained it on the First-day morning in the coach, with her bag on her knees and her steel rule lying across the bag.
     4	
     5	"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
     6	
     7	"Has a seat ever used it?" said Karis. "The recall."
     8	
     9	"In my time? Twice. Once to ask a guild master his mother's maiden name, which had been left off a form." The counsel's mouth moved at one corner. "Seats do not think of things late. They are very thorough about thinking of them early. But the rule stands, and we keep it, and in four days nobody will be able to say that the house was not where it was told to be."
    10	
    11	So they came down to Ostrand with trunks. Withrow rode in her own carriage and Bracken beside her with the seal. The coach carried Karis and Cael, as it always did, and with them Brom, whose panel sat in the same town on the same Fifth-day, and Lira, who had no business there at all and had got Rooke's leave in four words. *She's drilled in worse*, the card on the east hall door had said, and under it, smaller, *Bring her back sound*.
    12	
    13	Seln stayed on the hill. He had a schedule to post and a return to write, and he had stood at the wing's door that morning with his hands in his pockets and watched the coach go, as he might have watched any coach.
    14	
    15	The house's lodging was the wool-factors' guesting-house, in the lane behind the exchange. It was a tall narrow building of old brick with a gallery along its back, and it smelled of lanolin, as everything did in that quarter of the town. The factors kept it for buyers who came upriver at the sales. Between sales it stood three parts empty, and the woman who kept it had given the house its whole upper floor for four nights at a price that made Bracken take off his second pair of glasses and look at her.
    16	
    17	From the window of the room he was to share with Brom, Cael could see the green cupola over the roofs, and hear the bell under it. He could see, too, the upper window of the inn across the lane, where a man in a travelling coat sat with a cup and did not pretend to be doing anything but sitting there.
    18	
    19	"They've moved in," said Brom, behind him. He was unpacking, which for Brom meant taking four things out of a bag and squaring each of them on the press. "I saw the other one at the lane's end with the horses. They didn't want the walk down from the road foot every morning either."
    20	
    21	"They're keeping within the bell."
    22	
    23	"Everybody is." Brom laid his whetstone in its sock beside his razor. He had not brought the envelope; it was on his table on the hill, square to the edge, with its seal up. He had brought the summons and the two dockets and the schedule, and they were in the left pocket, and his hand went there now and came away. "Four days. I never thought I'd want a Fifth-day to hurry."
