   225	Cael had not known how large it was. He had seen it with thirty people in it and with two hundred, and never as it stood now, with the forty benches running back bare to the doors and the lot numbers showing in the floor between them. The high east windows laid four long bars of light across the boards. There was nobody on the steps outside, for nobody had been told. A return was posted in the porch after it was made.
   226	
   227	Eleven people. He counted them, because it took no time. Jent and his thin clerk. Withrow, the counsel, Karis, Bracken and himself. The seat's clerk. The two at the records table.
   228	
   229	On the platform the presiding chair stood behind its long table with a grey cover drawn over it.
   230	
   231	He looked at that for longer than he looked at anything else. For thirteen sittings there had been a hood in that place, and he had tried each time to learn something from how it sat. There was a cloth there now, hanging in folds, as there had been cloths on the five chairs below it before the third sitting. It told him exactly as much.
   232	
   233	Four of the five chairs were empty too. Ilsev had come alone.
   234	
   235	She stood up from the middle one. She was a spare woman of sixty or so with her grey hair cut short, in a plain coat, and she had her file open on her left forearm at the angle at which he had seen it for a year. She did not come forward. She stood where she had sat, and looked at the seat's clerk.
   236	
   237	"The evaluation seat makes its return," she said. "I shall read it at the pace of dictation. I ask that it be taken down as read, and not from my leaf afterward."
   238	
   239	"As read," said the clerk, and dipped his pen. At the records table the junior chair wrote the time.
   240	
   241	Beside Cael, Karis had opened the wallet. She had no table for ink in a wool yard, and had not needed one for a week. She took out the square of planed deal and a pencil, and set the pencil's point on a clean sheet.
   242	
   243	"The scope of this seat was minuted at the third sitting, in the presiding seat's words," said Ilsev. She spoke slowly, with a small pause at the end of every clause, and three pens went with her. "It has kept within them. Within that scope the evaluation seat is obliged to lay before the proceedings any matter upon the subject's file that would govern the conduct of an evaluation. There is one such matter. The file carries a designation. The petitioner has set it out in the second schedule to his petition, at the fourteenth line. A designation of that kind governs who must be present at an evaluation, and to what office its result is sent."
   244	
   245	She turned a leaf in her file.
   246	
   247	"This officer has twice referred that designation, in the proper form, to learn the office that entered it. She has received two returns. She will read both into the record, entire, as they came to her."
   248	
   249	Nothing in the empty room moved.
   250	
   251	"The first. *Return, from the chain of review, to the senior evaluation seat, on referral of the matter at Greyvane. Designation: Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*" The pause. "*Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*"
   252	
   253	Karis's pencil did not stop. Cael watched it go across the deal in her small upright hand, a word behind the voice.
   254	
   255	"The second. *Referral closed at registry level; no further inquiry authorized.*"
   256	
   257	Ilsev turned the leaf back.
   258	
   259	"And the note which this officer has written beside that designation upon every form she has filed concerning the subject, and which she now enters here. *Authorizing office: not traced.*"
   260	
   261	She lifted her eyes from the file. She did not look at the house's table, or at Jent, but at the grey cover on the presiding chair.
   262	
   263	"The evaluation seat notes the contradiction. The registry relies upon a designation to govern an evaluation, and the registry's own returns say that no office of record entered it. The evaluation seat makes no finding upon that. It is not within the scope minuted for this seat. This officer has been told that no further inquiry is authorized, and she makes none. She reads what she was sent." A last pause, no longer than the others. "That is the return."
   264	
   265	She sat down, and set the file at its angle.
   266	
   267	Cael looked up at the clock over the doors. Six minutes.
   268	
   269	He sat with his hands flat on the wood, and they were quite steady, and he was glad of that.
   270	
   271	*Designation predates file creation.*
   272	
   273	He had been fourteen. A room at Denvash, a desk, eleven seconds. Karis had laid it out for him on a night of frost, with her gloves on: something that was not the reading had looked for a column to put him in, and had found none, and had done the safe thing. It had written a word where an older name had been. And it had set a watch on him, at that same instant, before a clerk had so much as ruled a sheet with his name at the top.
   274	
   275	There had been no file yet. The registry had just said so. It had said so twice, under seal, in a clerk's round hand, and a careful woman with short grey hair had read it out at the pace of dictation in an empty wool exchange so that it would be taken down right.
   276	
   277	She did not know what it meant. He was as sure of that as he had been of the man at the station door. She had found a hole in the record and been told not to look into it, and she had done the one thing that was left inside her clearance, which was to say aloud, in public, that it was there.
   278	
   279	"Received from the evaluation seat," said the clerk, "and entered in the minute. A fair copy will stand in the public record before the ruling. Has either party an observation?"
   280	
   281	Jent rose. He looked, for the first time in a year, a little tired.
   282	
   283	"The petitioner set that designation out in his schedule because it is upon the file," he said. "He was not told its office either. He has no observation."
   284	
   285	"The respondent?"
   286	
   287	The counsel stood. "None," she said, and sat.
   288	
   289	That was all. The clerk shut his book.
   290	
   291	On the steps, in the empty street, Karis stopped with the deal board against her chest. She had the whole of it, in pencil, in seventeen lines, and she was looking at the eleventh.
   292	
   293	"That's the truest sentence anybody has entered about you in a year of sittings," she said, "and the woman who read it out has no idea what it says."
   294	
   295	She put the board into the wallet, and did up the strap.

