     1	# Chapter 53 — The Sunward Mark
     2	
     3	There was a light under the chancellor's door, and he had known there would be.
     4	
     5	He stood in the passage a moment with the satchel on his shoulder. It was past midnight, and the house was dark from the lodge to the library, and he had come down four flights and across the court to tell a woman a thing before a board could tell her. He had not thought about what he would say. He knocked on the frame, as he knocked on Brom's.
     6	
     7	"Come," said Withrow.
     8	
     9	She was at her desk with the lamp turned low and her hands on the blotter, a hand's width apart, and she was not writing. The two piles of letters were gone. The charter hung in its frame behind her, and under it the locked case stood open, and the folder from the case lay on the desk in front of her, shut.
    10	
    11	"Sit," she said. "You've come to tell me you are going, and you would rather I heard it from you than from Bracken's face in the morning. I have already seen Bracken's face. He came down your stair an hour ago and asked me for the key to the stationery press, and he is not a man who can ask for a key without my knowing what it is for."
    12	
    13	Cael sat down in the chair across the desk.
    14	
    15	"All of us," he said. "Before the year closes. By a door that's in the code, and in an old enrollment book. Karis found it in the term before the recess, and it leaves nothing in that folder."
    16	
    17	"*Resigned in good standing.*" She said it without surprise. "I know the door. I have known it for four days."
    18	
    19	He looked at her. He had seen her through two panes of glass, on the first evening in Ostrand, reach across a table and shut a book, and he had seen her face before a carriage door closed. He had put both away to look at later, and it was later now.
    20	
    21	"Then you should have the whole of it," said Withrow, "since you have brought me yours." She took a folded sheet from inside her coat and opened it on the blotter and turned it toward him. It was not the house's paper. There were four lines on it in the small capitals of the house's counsel, and each had a day written against it.
    22	
    23	*Ruling: the Fifth-day.*
    24	
    25	*Notice of date, to the Chancellor, by a district officer: upon the ruling, or the day after.*
    26	
    27	*Evaluation: the Fourth-day following. The last day of the academic year.*
    28	
    29	*Revocation takes effect: at that day's close.*
    30	
    31	"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
    32	
    33	"So it isn't five weeks."
    34	
    35	"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
    36	
    37	Five weeks had become five days. He heard the sentence in his head in the log voice, flat, and knew that he would write it down.
    38	
    39	"The counsel showed you this on the First-day," he said.
    40	
    41	"She did. She showed me the arithmetic, and then she showed me the section Karis found, and she recommended that I put it to you." Withrow took her hands off the blotter and folded them. "She was right to recommend it, and it was good advice. If the enrollee withdraws before the day, the order has nobody to stand in the frame and no house to produce him, and my folder shows a challenge answered and a house cleared. I have known since the First-day evening that the best thing that could happen to this academy was for you to walk out of it."
    42	
    43	"And you didn't say so."
    44	
    45	"No."
    46	
    47	The lamp ticked. He waited, because she was not a woman who left a thing half said.
    48	
    49	"Four chancellors advised me this term to let you go," said Withrow. "I answered every one of them in my own hand, and told them that the harm was done the day a house handed over an enrollee to spare itself. I believed it when I wrote it." She looked at him directly. "And then a small woman with a steel rule showed me that I need hand nobody over. I need only mention a door: a quiet word, in a guesting-house parlour, from a chancellor to an enrollee who has spent a year trying not to cost her anything. You'd have gone that night. Don't tell me you wouldn't. You'd have gone for my folder, and I should have done with one sentence what I refused to do under four riders."
    50	
    51	He did not tell her that he wouldn't, for it was true.
    52	
    53	"So I kept it," she said. "For four days, through a return and a ruling. I let you hear the hood say *revoked* without knowing how short the time was. I should like to say that it was easy and that I never wavered, and I can't; I sat in a carriage this afternoon and very nearly said it through the window." Her mouth moved a little. "I wanted it to be yours. If you went, I wanted you to go because you had priced it with your own people, at a table, with my need left out of the sum. And if you stayed, I meant to walk beside you into that frame-house and take what they wrote."
    54	
    55	"It wasn't left out," said Cael. "I've counted it since the first week."
    56	
    57	"I know. I watched you count it. But you put it there yourself, and I did not. That is the whole of the difference, and it is the only thing I have had to give you this year that was worth anything."
    58	
    59	He found that he could not answer. He had been keeping the largest thing he knew from her for half a year, and it had sat between them like a lie in every room; she had kept a thing from him for four days, and he saw now which of the two silences had been the kinder.
