    62	
    63	She opened the folder.
    64	
    65	She turned it on the desk so that it faced him, as she had on the night she told him to write down a word, and it was open at the same leaf. On the left was the entry for the eighth day, in Bracken's hand: the house a respondent before the seat at Ostrand, the subject of the proceedings, *Status: pending*. On the right the column was ruled, and still empty.
    66	
    67	"I showed you my column once," she said. "Four papers out of a drawer. Now the other one."
    68	
    69	She laid one finger on the empty ruling.
    70	
    71	"In the week after the close a clerk will come up our road with a stamp, and he will write here what the second head of the ruling says. *Respondent institution compliant; no finding.* I shall give him tea. I told you a year ago that the best day this folder could have was a clerk writing *sustained*, and I was wrong, for this is better. It is the cleanest remedy column I have seen against a contested entry in twenty years at the registry and all my years in this chair." She took the finger away. "And in Bracken's enrollment book, under your name, there will be five words in his hand. Under three other names as well, I gather, though I have not asked how many and I shall not."
    72	
    73	She shut the folder, quite gently, with one hand.
    74	
    75	"That is what a year of this comes to on paper. An enrollee was challenged. The house answered and was cleared. The enrollee left. Every inspector who opens that case for the next thirty years will start at the grant and turn forward, and that is what he will read, and he will draw the natural conclusion from it. He will think the boy saw which way it was going, and ran, and that the house was well rid of him."
    76	
    77	"It's what the form says."
    78	
    79	"It is. Forms say what happened and never why, and I have spent my life filling them in." Withrow stood up. She was a tall woman, and she stood very straight, and for the first time since the recess she did not look tired. "The record will say you left. I'll spend the rest of my tenure making the record say why."
    80	
    81	He looked up at her.
    82	
    83	"There is no form for taking a leaf out of that folder," she said. "I told you so. There is also no rule against putting one in. I have a closing in the registry's presses under an enrollee's name, which I can cite, and a magister's note for the seventy-sixth day. I have thirty-four chancellors who wrote to ask me what I would do, and who now owe me the courtesy of reading what I did. And I have a right-hand column in a great many places besides this one." She came round the end of the desk. "I do not know what took you up that stair tonight with four others. I am not a fool, and I can see that it was more than a ruling, and I am not going to ask. Bracken asks when. I find I do not want even that."
    84	
    85	"Chancellor—"
    86	
    87	"No. I said in the term that I'd be annoyed if you thanked me, and I should be." She had her hand on the door. "The captain came to me at supper to ask for the Crown yard tomorrow at the fourth bell. I told him the yard was Rooke's to give and the bout was yours, and I see by your face that he has since asked you. I shall be in the stand. I have sat under a rug in other people's halls for two years to watch my own fighters, and I should like to see one thing on my own oak before the year closes."
    88	
    89	She opened the door herself.
    90	
    91	He had never known her do it for anybody. A chancellor rang, or said *that will be all*, and one found one's own way out. She stood with the door held wide and the dark passage beyond it, as a district officer had stood at a station on the north road, and it was not the same thing at all.
    92	
    93	"Go to bed," said Withrow. "You have a floor tomorrow, and for once nobody has told you to look ordinary on it."
