    95	---
    96	
    97	He did not go to bed. There was a thing to write first, and the rule said it was to be written before, at its full weight, by a lamp, with nobody hurrying him.
    98	
    99	He sat at the sill with Hesk's clock losing its minutes on the shelf behind him and turned to a clean leaf. He had priced the eighth on a leaf like this, in the week of the rain, and four people had put their initials to it, and he had not thought then that he would ever price another. He wrote it slowly.
