   119	Brom's lamp was lit. He was sitting on the edge of his bed in his shirt with his forearms on his knees, and on the table under the window lay two papers. On the left was the district's finding, folded along its folds, with the printed slip tucked into it; on the right, a hand's breadth off, lay the envelope with the red seal, as he had squared it four weeks ago, and its seal had not so much as a crack across it.
   120	
   121	"I said I'd know what I was answering," said Brom, when he saw where Cael was looking. "I do. I find I needn't read the question." He took the book and read the leaf through at his own pace, and set his mark at the foot, the B with the bar. "They can lie there, the two of them. I've signed nothing and I've refused nothing. Somebody will find them when the room's cleared and wonder." He gave the book back. "He'll hit you hard. He hits harder than he looks."
   122	
   123	Karis was still in the reading room, on the floor, with a lantern. She read the leaf twice, and at *I'll price it as if it didn't* she made a small sound through her nose and wrote *K.* under Brom's mark.
   124	
   125	Lira's door was shut and there was no light under it. He knocked, and nobody answered, and after a moment he tore a corner from the back of a schedule and wrote on it against the wall: *Priced. It's in the book, on my sill. Come and read it.* He pushed it under the door.
   126	
   127	At the sill again he turned back a leaf and wrote the day's line under the ruling's three words, because it would not wait for morning.
   128	
   129	*Five weeks became five days.*
   130	
   131	He meant to sit up until she came, and he put his head down on his arm to rest his eyes.
   132	
   133	---
   134	
   135	Lira had the east hall to herself, and no lantern.
   136	
   137	She had not lit one. She knew the floor well enough by now to walk it blind, every cross and arrow of it, and the gallery shutters let in a little grey from the court. She stood at the south chalk with the key in her fist and her boots off.
   138	
   139	Somebody had painted this floor for her.
   140	
   141	She had never quite let herself say it in so many words. She had said *the program*, and *Rooke's floor*, and once, to Cael on a stair, *heavy*. But the plain fact was that a man had taken a hall, and had it planed, and sent to a river academy for a red book, and had a painter put four hundred marks on the boards, each one where a Wind fighter of exactly her reach would want it. Nobody had built anything to her height before. Fenmark had built a room for a girl it had imagined, and when she had turned out not to be that girl it had put her outside the room with her form correctly filled in.
   142	
   143	She ran the count, front foot and back foot and shoulders, and went on the fourth, a small low burst across the grey, and came down on the right. The boards took her and gave her back. She went again and came down on the left, and she had not needed to choose. That was the thing she had learned in this hall, on eleven tries one night and a thousand since: that the foot was not a question you asked.
   144	
   145	She stood on a painted cross and breathed.
   146	
   147	All her life she had wanted one thing from a room, which was to be let walk out of it standing up. She had been proud of that. She had said it to Cael on a road somewhere, as if it were a thing to be proud of, to want so little. And then a man with a board under his arm had built her a ladder, and she had found out in about a week that she wanted to climb it more than she had ever wanted anything, and had been ashamed, and then had stopped being ashamed.
   148	
   149	She was going to walk out of this room standing up, and nobody was putting her out. The door was open, and there was a sitting with her name fourth on a board, and a coach who had ruled a line under it so that it could not be wiped off. She could stay. She had turned that over on a stair in the records hall, honestly, watching Brom weigh paper: she could let four people go east, and be at a blue door in a fortnight in clean wraps.
   150	
   151	She had put her hand up second.
   152	
   153	It was the first room she had ever left that she would miss, and she thought that was probably what it was to have been given something.
   154	
   155	The red book lay on Rooke's table, where it always lay. There was a word in it under her name; he had written it on the day of the Blade, and then a second, and she had never seen either. She stood over the book in the dark with her hand flat on its cover, and did not open it. It was his, and whatever he had written, he had written of the program's entrant. She would tell him tomorrow, after the bout, in one sentence, to his face, for she owed him that much and he would want no more. If he chose to show her the page then, he would.
   156	
   157	She laid the key on the red book, and then took it up again. Not yet. There were five days, and she meant to drill on every one of them.
   158	
   159	There was a scrap of paper under her door when she came up, and his door, when she got to it, was not latched. He was asleep at the window with his head on his arm and the lamp burning down, and that foolish clock ticking on the shelf, behind the world as it always was. The book lay open under his elbow.
   160	
   161	She eased it out, and he did not stir. He slept like a person who had been sat up straight for a year.
   162	
   163	She read the leaf standing, by what was left of the lamp. She had read one of these before, in a week of rain, and had written across the bottom of it in capitals that nobody was to go in after him alone. That one had been about a woman on a road who meant to put him in a wagon; it had frightened her, and it had been simple. This one was not simple. She read down it, through the forearm and the lanes, and came to the part where the pen had plainly stopped and started again.
   164	
   165	*He isn't to be told.*
   166	
   167	Lira stood with the book in her hands.
   168	
   169	She had known what he could do since Ardenmere, when she was fifteen. She had never once thought of it as a thing taken from her; it had come out of him, and it was hers too, and she had been glad. But she had known. Nobody had ever kept it from her.
   170	
   171	The captain was going to stand on the oak tomorrow and be given the truest fight of his life, and one true thing would be kept back from him for as long as he lived. He had asked for the measurement, and he would get a great deal of it. She found that she minded for him, more than she had expected to; he had turned his back on a table of papers for her friend's sake on a birthday, and gone down a stair whistling.
   172	
   173	And she saw that Cael minded more than she did. He had written it down as a cost, at full weight, in ink, before he could be tempted to call it something else. That was the rule working, and it was what the rule was for.
   174	
   175	She took the pen from where it had rolled against the clock. Under Brom's barred letter and Karis's neat one she wrote *L.* Then she turned the pen in her fingers, and wrote one line more, across the foot, in the capitals she kept for such leaves.
   176	
   177	*HE ASKED. DON'T BE KIND.*
   178	
   179	She slid the book back under his elbow and turned the lamp down to save the oil.
   180	
   181	At the end of the corridor a window looked north over the roofs, and from it you could see the Crown yard, a black pit with a pale floor, and she stopped there because there was a light in it. A lantern stood on the faculty stand. By it she could make out the porter's ladder against the first of the four boards, and a short square man on the third rung with his sleeve rolled, writing with a lump of chalk.
   182	
   183	He wrote for a long time, for a man who used one word.
   184	
   185	He had not waited for the porter, or for morning. Somebody had come to his door at midnight and told him, and he had put on his coat and gone down to the yard in the dark and chalked it himself, before any officer of the house could wake up and wonder whether it was wise. She watched until he climbed down and held the lantern up to read it over, and then rubbed out one word with his cuff and wrote it again.
   186	
   187	She went to bed in her wraps. She thought she would not sleep, and she slept at once.
   188	
   189	---
   190	
   191	Cael read the board at first light, with a stiff neck and three sets of initials under his own.
   192	
   193	He had come down by the rim, alone, before the first bell. The oak lay below him in its pit, pale and dry, and on the wall above the faculty stand the first of the four black boards had been wiped of the year's afternoons, every one, and four lines stood on it in Rooke's square capitals.
   194	
   195	*THE CROWN YARD. THE FOURTH BELL.*
   196	
   197	*BLADE, IRON SIX. THE ENROLLEE.*
   198	
   199	*ROOKE'S RULE: FOUR EXCHANGES, A TOUCH OR NONE IN EACH. TWO ENDS IT.*
   200	
   201	*THE HILL AND THE TOWN.*
   202	
   203	He read the last line twice. Rooke had closed a floor to observers in the year's seventh week, with a chain and a card, and had kept a ledger with *none* in it ever since; and now he had chalked an invitation to a valley.
   204	
   205	By the second bell the valley was coming.
   206	
   207	Cael watched it from the north wall, because there was nothing else he could usefully do. The ferry came across low in the water and went back empty and came again. The bluff road had people on every switchback: wool-factors in their good coats, and carters, and a file of district clerks who had found that they had business on the hill. The man who had bought a second press was there, and so was the woman who kept the guesting-house, with a basket. The lake house's blue-grey wagon had not gone home after the ruling, and it came up at a walk with a lath in a canvas sleeve sticking out of the back.
   208	
   209	The lodge-man had his book out on a trestle under the arch and was making every one of them sign it. *Callers, sign and wait*, said his board, and he had kept that gate since before Bracken, and saw no reason why a ruling should alter it.
   210	

