   211	Two men in travelling coats came up among the rest, and waited their turn at the trestle like anybody, and took the pen, and wrote. Cael was too far off to see what they put. They did not look up at the wall, and one of them had a walking stick he did not need.
   212	
   213	He had no line to read at his chalk today, and he found that strange. For a year and more he had read one before every bout: what to take late, and what to give, and the figure it was meant to buy. He had the rotation's book in his coat from habit, and at the north wall he took it out and opened it at the day.
   214	
   215	*Sixth-day. The Crown yard. Ephram. All of it.*
   216	
   217	There was nothing under that. What it bought and what it spent were on another leaf, in another book, with capitals across the foot. He shut the rotation and did not open it again.
   218	
   219	---
   220	
   221	At the fourth bell he went down the steps onto the oak, and counted the yard one last time.
   222	
   223	It was sixty feet by forty, laid so close that a fingernail would not go between the boards, and sunk four feet below the ground, with three short flights of steps, of which he had come down the east one. The stone tiers went up from the rim on three sides, with eleven hundred seats in them and a little over; he had counted them at dawn in his first week on the hill, when they were empty, and had felt them on the back of his neck.
   224	
   225	They were not empty now. He counted by tens and by rows, as he had counted benches in a wool exchange, and gave it up at twelve hundred, where the stone ran out and the people did not. They stood along the rim behind the top tier, and sat on the steps, and a row of first-years had got onto the wall of the lecture range and were dangling their legs.
   226	
   227	On the fourth side was the faculty stand. Withrow sat in the registrar's chair at its middle, upright, with her hands in her lap, and Bracken stood behind her. Gault was at the stand's end with his instrument case set very straight beside him, though there was nothing in a bout for it to measure. At the long table Rooke stood with his glass and his board, and his clerk sat at his elbow with two books open, and the house's healer had the bench below with her bag.
   228	
   229	He found his own people without looking for them. They were on the east tier, low down: Brom, with his arms folded, and Karis beside him with her hands under her, and Lira between the Current girl and the Stone. The assistant sat behind them, and behind her were two benches of Ephram's cohort, who had been told nothing about cheering today. At the end of the stand, by the porter's boards, where the year's names stood in their columns, a man in a dark coat stood with his hands in his pockets, and nobody would have looked at him twice.
   230	
   231	High on the west tier, at the very top, sat a grey man with a walking stick across his knees and a pencil.
   232	
   233	*Let him write*, Cael thought, and it surprised him how little it weighed.
   234	
   235	Ephram was on the oak already. He stood in the middle of it in his greys with the lath in his right hand, point down, and when Cael came off the steps he turned and walked to a chalk. He did not go to the south one. It was the fourth bell, with the sun come round over the lecture range, and from the south chalk a man had it at his back. Ephram walked the whole length of the floor to the north mark and turned there, and stood with the light full in his face.
   236	
   237	It was the worse mark, and any first-year knew it. A captain who had headed the Iron column for three years could have taken either, and nobody would have said a word.
   238	
   239	He was leaving himself no excuse. Cael understood it as he walked to the south chalk with the sun warm between his shoulders: whatever Ephram learned in the next four exchanges, he meant to have learned it against every disadvantage he could arrange, so that there would be nothing afterward to set against it.
   240	
   241	Rooke came to the edge of the stand.
   242	
   243	"Rooke's rule," he said. He did not raise his voice, and twelve hundred people heard him. "Four exchanges. A touch or none. Two ends it. I call a guard a guard." He looked along the oak, from one chalk to the other. "Exchange."
   244	
   245	His hand came down.
   246	
   247	---
   248	
   249	Ephram came off the north mark at a walk.
   250	
   251	Cael knew the walk. He had stood in front of it for a season on this floor, calling the weight aloud: every angle, every pace, the lath low and never thrown. The old read went out through his boots into sprung oak and came back with Ephram in it, early, as it always did. The weight was forward and a little left, the shoulders had not dropped, and the back heel was down. It was the front foot only, then, and the thing to do was wait.
   252	
   253	The heel came up. Cael slipped right and outside, as he had slipped a hundred times, and the lath went by his hip.
   254	
   255	It did not stop, and that was new. The first cut had not finished before Ephram's front heel was down again, a pace on, and a second cut was already beginning out of the end of the first, a different way in, flat, at the ribs. Heel, hip, edge. He could not take the first one back and had not tried to; he had only built the second on top of it, as a mason lays a course.
   256	
   257	Cael gave ground with one burst, small and low, spent short so that from a tier it would look like a quick step.
   258	
   259	And the third was coming out of the second. He had watched this from the rim on a First-day, slow, against nobody: three in a row, each a different way in, each begun before the last had ended. He read the heel go down for the third, and the weight, and where the cut would finish, and he knew, a half beat early, exactly where he needed to be.
   260	
   261	He waited the half beat.
   262	
   263	He did not choose to. That was the shame of it, and he felt it even as it happened. A year of Fifth-days in wing three had put the waiting into his body as a drill puts a landing into a foot. Twelve hundred people were looking down, and a grey man with a pencil, and the coat was on him before he had so much as thought of it. He stood in his own bout and waited, so that it would look as if he had found the answer when a good Iron would have found it.
   264	
   265	The hip never came.
   266	
   267	Heel, and then the edge. Ephram had cut the middle out of it, and the lath was simply there, a beat sooner than three years of watching had taught Cael's eyes to expect, and it lay across his left forearm from the wrist to the elbow like a bar of cold iron. Then it was hot.
   268	
   269	"Touch," said Rooke. "Blade."
   270	
   271	The tiers made a noise, though not much of one. They had seen a good fighter take a touch from a better, as they had seen it ninety-eight times.
   272	
   273	Ephram did not lift the lath in salute. He looked at Cael along it for a moment, with the sun in his eyes and no pleasure at all in his plain face, and then he turned and walked back to the north chalk.
   274	
   275	Cael walked to the south. His forearm had begun to throb in a line. A Blade's edge was not the wood's; he had written that last night as a guess, and it was a guess no longer.
   276	
   277	*Take the first late*, the rotation had said for a year.
   278	
   279	"Exchange," said Rooke.
   280	
   281	Ephram did not move.
   282	
   283	He stood on the north chalk with his feet together. He turned the lath over once in his hand, and set its tip on the oak, and folded both hands on the grip, as a man leans on a spade. The yard waited. Rooke's hand had come down, and the glass was turned, and the sand was running.
   284	
   285	"That's ninety-nine," said Ephram.
   286	
   287	He did not shout it. He said it across forty feet of oak in his captain's voice, and it carried to the rim.
   288	
   289	"I've ninety-eight of those on three sheets. I don't want another. I said what I'd do." He nodded at the lath under his hands. "It's down. I'll stand here till the sand's out, and the next glass, and the one after that. You can touch me twice whenever you like, and they'll put it on the board that you won."
   290	
   291	A murmur went round the tiers and died. On the stand Rooke had not stirred, and he had not called it either.
   292	
   293	Cael stood on the south chalk with the sun on his back, and he could feel every one of them above him. He had felt eleven hundred empty seats on his neck at dawn in his first week, and it was nothing to this. Withrow sat in the registrar's chair, and Gault had signed two years of his figures, and a coat held a pencil at the top of the west tier. And there were four hundred people he had eaten beside since he came up the road, who knew him as a quiet enrollee with a provision and a decent read.
   294	
   295	*With a table and a yard full of people*, Lira had said, sitting on a painted floor. *I know what their face is going to do.*
   296	
   297	Five hands had gone up for this, and he had priced it. He looked down the length of the oak at a man leaning on a lath with the sun in his eyes, who had ruled a red line in the first month he knew him and had waited three years to be shown the far side of it.
   298	
   299	He did not take the coat off. He let it fall.
   300	
   301	It was easier than it had been in the east hall, and he had not thought that possible. Everything he owned came awake at once, under an open sky, in front of everybody, and for the first time since Norhold there was no part of him standing guard over any other part.
   302	
   303	He did not wait.
   304	
   305	The Wind first, because it was the oldest: low and flat off the left foot, straight down the middle of the floor. The oak took the landing and threw him on, as it was built to, and he was in the air again before the beat had finished. Three. Ephram's hands had come off the grip, and he had the lath up, and his heel was going down, and the read had all of it, deep, to the hips, a full beat before it happened.
   306	
   307	Cael put the push into the oak under that heel.
   308	
   309	It was not a flat hand, which was all he had ever shown on the bluff. This was the push at fighting load, the whole shove of it, through his palm and down, and the air over a square yard of floor went solid and then gave. Ephram's front heel came down on a board that had jumped, and the first beat of his cut landed crooked. A Blade could not take it back. The hip followed the crooked heel, and the edge followed the hip, wide, a yard from anybody.
   310	
   311	The tiers had gone silent.
   312	
   313	Four. He went past the wide cut on the outside, and Ephram turned on the miss faster than a man of his size ought to turn. A line was already there. He had not cut it; he had turned his wrist, and the lath lay level across the place where Cael's next landing would come down, because he read lines, and knew what would cross one a breath before it crossed.
   314	
   315	Cael saw the lath waiting and took a fifth burst out of the air above it, sideways, off nothing but the landing beat of the fourth, and came down ten feet clear with his hip beginning to write. Five on the oak, and the day's free six were nearly gone.
   316	
   317	Ephram was grinning. Cael saw it, across the floor, with the sun full on it, and he had never seen that face grin on the oak in three years.
   318	
   319	Then he asked the air to lean.
   320	
   321	He had done it on a stone floor at Norhold with eight thousand people watching, and on a chalk road, and in a painted hall at night, and he had never done it here. He set his feet and asked, and a lane went down across the Crown yard from his chalk to the space behind Ephram's left shoulder, fifteen feet of it, shoulder-wide. His ears closed and opened. The dust in the joints of the oak woke and ran, pale, along the line.
   322	
   323	Somebody on the east tier screamed, with delight, and he thought it was the Current girl.
   324	
   325	The read had gone out into the lane, as it must. He took it back off the air and gave it to the man, as he had learned to at a price, and then he stepped into the downhill and it carried him a third faster than any feet.
   326	
   327	The lath was lying across the lane's end.
   328	
   329	He saw it with a beat to spare, and understood it in the same instant. A lane was a line. It was the longest, plainest line anybody had ever laid on that oak, with dust running down it to show the way, and Ephram had not needed to know what it was. He had read where it went, and had put his edge at the end of it and stood there, waiting, with his heel down.
   330	
   331	Cael came out of the lane into a cut that was already committed, and burst out of it. Six. Straight up and back, off the lane's own speed, with the edge passing under his boots near enough to feel, and he landed badly, with the hip shouting.
   332	
   333	And then the bill came in.
   334	
   335	It was the warm hand over the inside of his skull, and everything went slow. He began the count, because it was the only thing that came when he called. *One.*
   336	
   337	Ephram did not know about the ten counts; nobody on the bluff did but three people on a tier. But he was the best Iron on the hill and he could see a man who had gone a beat late, and he came in.
   338	
   339	*Two. Three.* Three cuts in a chain, heel and hip and edge. Cael asked for the Wind and it came after he had asked, and he got out from under the first by the width of a sleeve. *Four.* The second he took on his raised left forearm, with the give open, and it opened late, and the edge went through it. *Five.* He had been right about that too. The give was a cup, and a cup holds a blow, and this was not a blow; it was a line of cold drawn across the bruise that was already there.
   340	
   341	"Guard," said Rooke.
   342	
   343	*Six. Seven.* The third was the short one, with no hip in it. He saw the heel go down, and he knew, and he could do nothing with the knowing for a whole slow beat. He spent a burst he could not afford. Seven. It was one past what the oak gave free, and it took him out sideways along the rim wall with the lath's wind on his neck.
   344	
   345	*Eight.*
   346	
   347	Ephram turned and came on, breathing through his mouth. He had the sun behind him now and Cael had it in his eyes; they had changed ends without either of them choosing it.
   348	
   349	*Nine. Ten.*
   350	
   351	At eleven the hand lifted off the inside of his head, and everything answered when he called.
   352	
   353	He had not felt the pull. He noticed that, with the sweat running into his eyes: no wrong thing had answered in all of that, on a full floor, at this weight. The right things had come late and then they had come. It was the bill, and not the flag.
   354	
   355	He set his feet, and Ephram set his. The lath came up, and the heel began to go down.
   356	
   357	"Time," said Rooke. "No touch."
   358	
   359	The sand had run out.
   360	
   361	For a breath there was no sound in the Crown yard at all. Twelve hundred people had watched a quiet enrollee with a provision go down the middle of the oak on a river of dust, and they had not yet agreed with one another about what they had seen.
   362	
   363	Ephram lowered the lath.
   364	
   365	He was soaked, and his chest was going like a bellows, and there was a look on his face that Cael had never seen on it, on the oak or off. It was the face of a man who has been adding a long column for three years and has reached the foot of it and found that the sum was larger than the paper.
   366	
   367	He looked up at the tiers for a moment, as if the three sheets were pinned there, with the red line he had ruled across them and the ninety-eight dots that had never touched it. Then he looked back, and laughed, once, out of what breath he had.
   368	
   369	"There it is. Three years. Again."

