+# Movement 008 source-distance read
+
+## Verdict
+
+**Pass: 2 genuine tracked passages.** The movement is independently told at scene scale. One
+unprotected sentence in Chapter 49 keeps a distinctive source formulation, and one short
+interpretive passage in Chapter 54 follows the source's reading of the Blade notice in the same
+order. Every other resemblance is protected wording, an exact packet/BOOK_MAP line, a required
+fact or event receiving a new telling, or common prose.
+
+Counting unit: one continuous manuscript passage following one source sentence, or one tightly
+unified source sentence-cluster, counts as one passage. When one source sentence becomes several
+manuscript sentences, it is one **split** passage. Scene numbers follow the manuscript's `---`
+divisions.
+
+The requested calibration command returned **0% skeleton / 10% close** overall across 1,863
+sentences. By chapter it returned: Ch48 0%/9%; Ch49 2%/13%; Ch50 0%/10%; Ch51 0%/10%; Ch52
+1%/9%; Ch53 0%/10%; Ch54 0%/10%. Its displayed exact/high pairs are chiefly the protected
+certification line, Withrow line, and BOOK_MAP spine line. The two findings below come from the
+side-by-side read, not from treating the probe's close band as findings.
+
+| Manuscript chapter | Scene counts (in manuscript order) | Chapter total |
+|---|---|---:|
+| 48 | S1 room/count: 0; S2 Jent: 0; S3 counsel/leave: 0; S4 Ephram: 0; S5 rehearsal: 0 | **0** |
+| 49 | S1 coach: 0; S2 opening/list: 0; S3 discretion/history: 1; S4 remedy/unsaid slip: 0; S5 close/entries: 0; S6 aftermath: 0 | **1** |
+| 50 | S1 call days: 0; S2 parlour: 0; S3 wool press: 0; S4 supplemental: 0; S5 station: 0; S6 Ilsev's return: 0 | **0** |
+| 51 | S1 copyists: 0; S2 porch: 0; S3 Havel's six: 0; S4 Brom's panel: 0; S5 ruling: 0; S6 steps: 0; S7 records hall: 0 | **0** |
+| 52 | S1 council frame: 0; S2 frame: 0; S3 cache: 0; S4 Brom/Bracken: 0; S5 pricing: 0; S6 Ephram/vote: 0 | **0** |
+| 53 | S1 Withrow's arithmetic: 0; S2 other column: 0; S3 price: 0; S4 Lira: 0; S5 board/arrivals: 0; S6 yard/marks: 0; S7 exchanges one and two: 0 | **0** |
+| 54 | S1 third exchange: 0; S2 fourth exchange: 0; S3 notice/verdicts: 1; S4 emptying yard: 0; S5 boards: 0; S6 log/pull/empty yard: 0 | **1** |
+| **Movement** |  | **2** |
+
+## Tracked passages
+
+| chapter:line | Manuscript opening words | Source sentence it follows (file:line plus opening words) | Kind |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 49:83 | In all that record these sections have never once been turned… | `chapter-18.md:31` — “In four hundred years… the integrity provisions have never once been turned on a person for what he is.” | near-verbatim |
+| 54:154 | He read it through twice… | `chapter-20.md:96–100` — “He read the last field twice… Every entry in the log was adversarial… This was a machine's grammar bending to describe a fight…” | varied |
+
+The Chapter 49 finding is narrower than the required ninety-one/seven/conduct argument. The plan
+requires that historical result; it does not require the source sentence's distinctive sequence
+*never once / been turned / on a person / for what he is*, which the manuscript retains with only
+`upon` for `on` and a changed lead-in.
+
+The Chapter 54 finding begins after the protected notice. The manuscript reads the notice, isolates
+its last field, contrasts the prior Gold and road acquisitions, then interprets this fight as
+earnest without harm and the notice as having a word for that difference. That is the source's
+same interpretive sequence, though the sentences and examples are compressed and varied. The
+notice itself is Tier A and is not part of the count.
+
+## Tier B independence check
+
+These passages remain excluded from the tracked count because BOOK_MAP section 8 protects the
+whole load-bearing text, including the author's composition around the supplied words. The
+independence judgments below address the requested source comparison, not whether they are allowed.
+
+1. **The ruling's five heads — independent.** Manuscript `chapter-51.md:177–191` and source
+   `chapter-18.md:109–111` necessarily share the packet's legal holdings and statutory vocabulary.
+   The manuscript adds the record/certificate head, separates lawfulness from institutional
+   compliance, moves revocation before the re-evaluation order, and omits the source's
+   description-versus-evaluation sentence. Its sentence architecture and operative order are new.
+
+2. **Ephram's speech around “Still glad.” — not fully independent.** Manuscript
+   `chapter-54.md:260–268` rebuilds most of the speech around Ephram's chart, the short measure, and
+   the shoulder. Its closing instruction, however — “Go and be whatever that was, somewhere
+   there's room for it” — is a near-verbatim variation of source `chapter-20.md:120`, “Go be it
+   somewhere with more room.” The shared distinctive imperative prevents an independent verdict
+   for the whole speech.
+
+3. **The ninth log entry after its first seven words — not fully independent.** After the protected
+   opening at manuscript `chapter-54.md:292`, the first new sentence says that Ephram asked for the
+   fight, not the acquisition, and gave it without knowing. Source `chapter-20.md:130` makes the
+   same consent distinction in the same local sequence: he consented to the fight, not what it fed,
+   and does not know what he gave. The later mechanism and injury accounting are new, but that
+   opening continuation is a varied carryover, so the composed entry is not wholly independent.
+
+## Exclusions checked
+
+- Exact Tier A/B wording was excluded: the certification line, ruling, Blade notice, Withrow's
+  record line, boxes exchange, Seln sentence, “Still glad.” speech, and ninth entry.
+- Exact packet/BOOK_MAP lines were excluded, including Jent's syllable line, the Ilsev return
+  wording, “You don't file a grievance…”, “Turns out it doesn't live here,” “Five weeks became five
+  days,” “There it is. Three years. Again.”, “Both. Enter both.”, Lira's first-time sentence, and
+  “Good room.”
+- The closings, four call days, district-station girl, Ilsev/Havel windows, five ruling heads,
+  three roads, priced departures, last-bout exchanges, verdict order, boards ritual, nondisclosure,
+  first pull, unused Anchor, and forms on Bracken's counter are plan-required facts or events. They
+  are excluded where the manuscript supplies new scene construction, causality, physical business,
+  or beat order.
+- Recurring gestures such as hands flat on a table, looking at a paper, squaring a leaf, and a room
+  going quiet were treated as common prose unless they carried the source sentence's substantive
+  sequence.
+
+## Dense-scene remedies
+
+None. No scene contains more than one genuine tracked passage. Chapter 49 scene 3 and Chapter 54
+scene 3 each have one line-level repair candidate; neither needs a new entry point or reordered
+scene on source-distance grounds. The protected Tier B convergence in Chapter 54 scenes 5–6 is
+reported above but does not make those scenes densely tracked under the requested counting rules.

